{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CAdNCbk2vikAFQK4mUTYtm","type":"tool_result","content":"# ACL Rolling Review Form Structure\n\n## Scoring Dimensions\n\n### 1. Reviewer Confidence (1-5 scale)\n- **5**: \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully\"\n- **4**: \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully\"\n- **3**: \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something\"\n- **2**: \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely that I missed some details\"\n- **1**: \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand\"\n\n### 2. Soundness (1-5 scale with .5 increments)\n- **5**: \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen\"\n- **4**: \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all claims\"\n- **3**: \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for main claims\"\n- **2**: \"Poor: Some main claims are not sufficiently supported\"\n- **1**: \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough\"\n\n### 3. Excitement (1-5 scale with .5 increments)\n- **5**: \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others\"\n- **4**: \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others\"\n- **3**: \"Interesting: I might mention some points\"\n- **2**: \"Potentially Interesting: does not resonate with me\"\n- **1**: \"Not Exciting: does not resonate with me\"\n\n### 4. Overall Assessment (1-5 scale with .5 increments)\n- **5**: Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- **4**: Conference acceptance\n- **3**: Findings acceptance\n- **2**: Resubmit next cycle\n- **1**: Do not resubmit\n\n### 5. Reproducibility (1-5 scale)\n- **5**: \"They could easily reproduce the results\"\n- **4**: \"They could mostly reproduce the results\"\n- **3**: \"They could reproduce with some difficulty\"\n- **2**: \"They would be hard pressed to reproduce\"\n- **1**: \"They would not be able to reproduce\"\n\n### 6. Datasets Value (1-5 scale)\n- **5**: Enabling\n- **4**: Useful\n- **3**: Potentially useful\n- **2**: Documentary\n- **1**: No usable datasets\n\n### 7. Software Value (1-5 scale)\n- **5**: Enabling\n- **4**: Useful\n- **3**: Potentially useful\n- **2**: Documentary\n- **1**: No usable software\n\n### 8. Author Identity Knowledge (1-5 scale)\n- **5**: Know/can guess author from double-blind violation\n- **4**: Know from preprint or workshop\n- **3**: Know from submission contents\n- **2**: Know from social media/talks\n- **1**: No educated guess\n\n## Required Fields\n- Paper Summary\n- Summary of Strengths\n- Summary of Weaknesses\n- Comments/Suggestions/Typos\n- Limitations and Societal Impact\n- Ethical Concerns\n- Needs Ethics Review (yes/no with justification)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3934af1-82f0-438e-bd39-6838b74091c7","uuid":"67682fc4-0207-4dc3-a91d-90c0438a3074","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:14:41.828Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":25365,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ACL Rolling Review Form Structure\n\n## Scoring Dimensions\n\n### 1. Reviewer Confidence (1-5 scale)\n- **5**: \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully\"\n- **4**: \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully\"\n- **3**: \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something\"\n- **2**: \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely that I missed some details\"\n- **1**: \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand\"\n\n### 2. Soundness (1-5 scale with .5 increments)\n- **5**: \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen\"\n- **4**: \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all claims\"\n- **3**: \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for main claims\"\n- **2**: \"Poor: Some main claims are not sufficiently supported\"\n- **1**: \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough\"\n\n### 3. Excitement (1-5 scale with .5 increments)\n- **5**: \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others\"\n- **4**: \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others\"\n- **3**: \"Interesting: I might mention some points\"\n- **2**: \"Potentially Interesting: does not resonate with me\"\n- **1**: \"Not Exciting: does not resonate with me\"\n\n### 4. Overall Assessment (1-5 scale with .5 increments)\n- **5**: Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- **4**: Conference acceptance\n- **3**: Findings acceptance\n- **2**: Resubmit next cycle\n- **1**: Do not resubmit\n\n### 5. Reproducibility (1-5 scale)\n- **5**: \"They could easily reproduce the results\"\n- **4**: \"They could mostly reproduce the results\"\n- **3**: \"They could reproduce with some difficulty\"\n- **2**: \"They would be hard pressed to reproduce\"\n- **1**: \"They would not be able to reproduce\"\n\n### 6. Datasets Value (1-5 scale)\n- **5**: Enabling\n- **4**: Useful\n- **3**: Potentially useful\n- **2**: Documentary\n- **1**: No usable datasets\n\n### 7. Software Value (1-5 scale)\n- **5**: Enabling\n- **4**: Useful\n- **3**: Potentially useful\n- **2**: Documentary\n- **1**: No usable software\n\n### 8. Author Identity Knowledge (1-5 scale)\n- **5**: Know/can guess author from double-blind violation\n- **4**: Know from preprint or workshop\n- **3**: Know from submission contents\n- **2**: Know from social media/talks\n- **1**: No educated guess\n\n## Required Fields\n- Paper Summary\n- Summary of Strengths\n- Summary of Weaknesses\n- Comments/Suggestions/Typos\n- Limitations and Societal Impact\n- Ethical Concerns\n- Needs Ethics Review (yes/no with justification)","durationMs":7570,"url":"https://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018jX7ZbZ4yYzS65TuZaZTuM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I now have the ARR review form with exact scoring scales. Let me summarize:\n\n- **Overall Assessment**: 1-5 scale with 0.5 increments\n  - 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n  - 4 = Conference acceptance\n  - 3 = Findings acceptance\n  - 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n  - 1 = Do not resubmit\n\n- **Soundness**: 1-5 with 0.5 increments\n\n\n- **Excitement**: 1-5 with 0.5 increments\n- **Reproducibility**: 1-5\n- **Confidence**: 1-5\n\nSince we're targeting EMNLP 2026 Findings, I need an overall score of 3. Now I'll set up the review process with two reviewers—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then walk through the four-step roundtable discussion to reach consensus.\n\nI'm going to launch both reviewers as parallel agents for the independent review phase, then handle the cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis steps myself. Let me prepare detailed prompts for each reviewer that include the paper content, the ARR scoring rubric, their specific focus areas, and the review context for assessing experimental coverage. I need to extract and organize the paper content from the PDF I've read—it's an 8-page paper titled \"Why Calib...\"\n\nNow I'm reviewing the key findings: the paper introduces a 4-mechanism taxonomy of calibration failure modes in LLM user simulators, testing both prompt-based and training-based calibration approaches on different model sizes. The failures range from global behavior suppression and cross-dimension interference to overcorrection rebound and DPO-BCP interaction reversal, with all four modes showing greater severity at the smaller model size. There's also a safety-efficiency tradeoff dimension to consider.\n\nLooking at the experimental validation, the bootstrap confidence intervals support the main claims, and the planned experiments for 14B DPO+BCP combinations are already reflected in the paper's tables. The main limitations are the focus on a single model family and the retail-only domain for τ-bench, but the core findings appear well-substantiated.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1177,"cache_read_input_tokens":53755,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1177},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3934af1-82f0-438e-bd39-6838b74091c7","uuid":"ce10a707-efe7-4a98-85f6-93d959c532b9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018jX7ZbZ4yYzS65TuZaZTuM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ARR review form loaded. Scoring rubric: Overall Assessment 1–5 (0.5 increments), where 5=Award, 4=Main conference, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit, 1=Do not resubmit. Soundness and Excitement also 1–5 with 0.5 increments.\n\nLaunching R1 (Methods+Experiments) and R2 (Novelty+Related Work) in parallel for Step 1 independent reviews."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1177,"cache_read_input_tokens":53755,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1177},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3934af1-82f0-438e-bd39-6838b74091c7","uuid":"543b2d2a-4fe8-4e4b-a3e3-65d9a8ac5b8e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018jX7ZbZ4yYzS65TuZaZTuM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011M3hibfofJrMQJmLC5Zuug","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 Findings submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Produce an independent review.\n\n## Scoring Rubric (ARR / EMNLP)\n- **Overall Assessment** (1–5, 0.5 increments): 5=Award (top 2.5%), 4=Main conference accept, 3=Findings accept, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n- **Soundness** (1–5, 0.5 increments): 5=Most thorough, 4=Sufficient support for all claims, 3=Sufficient for main claims, 2=Some main claims unsupported, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1–5, 0.5 increments): 5=Highly exciting, recommend to others; 4=Exciting, mention to others; 3=Interesting; 2=Potentially interesting; 1=Not exciting\n- **Reproducibility** (1–5): 5=Easily reproduce, 4=Mostly reproduce, 3=Some difficulty, 2=Hard pressed, 1=Cannot reproduce\n- **Confidence** (1–5): 5=Positive evaluation correct; 4=Quite sure; 3=Pretty sure; 2=Willing to defend but may have missed details; 1=Not my area\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Why Calibration Fails: A Taxonomy of Failure Modes in Dialect-Aware LLM User Simulation\"\n8 pages, anonymous submission.\n\nThe paper studies why calibration of LLM-based user simulators fails when trying to correct dialect-dependent behavioral biases (AAVE and Indian English vs Standard American English). Two calibration strategies are tested: Behavioral Calibration Prompting (BCP, inference-time) and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO, training-time). Both fail.\n\nThe authors propose a 4-mechanism taxonomy of calibration failures:\n- **FM1: Global behavior suppression** — BCP collapses task completion at 7B (retention 19.3% vs ≥80% threshold). At 14B, disappears (>97% retention).\n- **FM2: Cross-dimension interference** — targeting one behavioral dimension perturbs others. Targeting question_rate causes −25.6% relative politeness drop (CI [−30.1, −20.8]). Persists at both scales (attenuated at 14B to −8.0%).\n- **FM3: Overcorrection rebound** — extreme calibration targets cause opposite-direction shifts at 7B (+7.23pp above baseline, CI [3.8, 10.6]). Disappears at 14B.\n- **FM4: DPO–BCP interaction reversal** — DPO reverses BCP calibration direction at 7B (reversal swing +15.8pp, CI [10.2, 21.8], 10/12 directional consistency, p=0.006). At 14B, attenuated (4/12, CI includes zero [−1.6, 5.9]). Cross-scale attenuation 14.7pp is significant (CI [8.1, 21.6]).\n\nKey finding: \"safety–efficacy gap\" — scaling eliminates destructive side effects (FM1, FM3) but doesn't improve calibration effectiveness (H2 fails at both scales).\n\n### Experimental Setup\n- τ-bench retail domain (16-tool customer service)\n- Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct\n- 3 behavioral dimensions: task completion, politeness rate, question rate\n- 3 hypotheses: H1 (dialect sensitivity ≥5% relative diff), H2 (gap reduction ≥30%), H3 (no harm, ≥80% completion retention)\n- DPO: 1,434 dialect-calibrated preference pairs, LoRA r=16 on q_proj/v_proj, FP16\n- 7B: 1×RTX 4090, ~1.4h. 14B: 2×RTX 4090 TP=2, ~7.5h\n- Bootstrap: B=10,000, episode-level resampling, BCa correction\n- Per-run evaluations: 4 runs × 6 variants × 20 tasks = 480 episodes (for multi-run)\n- Single-run experiments: n=19-20 per variant\n\n### Tables in paper\n- Table 1: FM4 per-cell gap direction at 7B (3 variants × 4 runs = 12 cells, 10/12 expansion)\n- Table 2: Bootstrap 95% BCa CIs for 5 core effects (all exclude zero)\n- Table 3: DPO training metrics 14B vs 7B\n- Table 4: 14B DPO+BCP per-run evaluation (4 runs)\n- Table 5: Cross-scale FM4 reversal comparison\n- Table 6: Scale-dependent failure severity matrix\n\n### Limitations stated by authors\n- Single model family (Qwen2.5) — Llama-3-8B and Mistral-7B controls got 5% and 0% completion\n- τ-bench retail domain only; FM2/FM3 may be task-dependent\n- Modest sample sizes (n=19-20 per variant single-run, 80 multi-run)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes:\n1. **Bootstrap CI for Key Comparisons (plan_005)**: B=10,000, BCa correction, 5 core statistics. CPU-only ~2h. Uses existing data. → Appears fully covered in Table 2.\n2. **14B DPO + BCP (plan_006)**: Phase A: DPO fine-tuning 14B (LoRA r=16, FP16, TP=2, 2×4090, ~7.5h, 3 epochs). Phase B: BCP evaluation (4 runs × 6 variants × 20 tasks = 480 episodes, ~5h). → Appears fully covered in Tables 3-5.\n\nBoth experiments appear reflected in the paper.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\nEvaluate:\n1. **Experimental design rigor**: Are the hypotheses well-defined? Are the experimental conditions sufficient? Are controls adequate?\n2. **Statistical methodology**: Is bootstrap with BCa appropriate? Are sample sizes adequate? Any statistical concerns?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Enough detail to replicate? Hardware, hyperparameters, data?\n4. **Internal validity**: Could confounds explain the results? Is the causal reasoning sound?\n5. **Experimental coverage**: Are there obvious missing experiments? Are ablations needed?\n6. **Tables and quantitative presentation**: Are results clearly presented? Any concerns about the numbers?\n\n## Output Format\nProduce your review in this exact structure:\n\n### Paper Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted, 4-6 points)\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, 4-6 points)\n\n### Questions for Authors (bulleted, 2-4)\n\n### Missing Experiments or Ablations (bulleted)\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X.X\n- Excitement: X.X\n- Reproducibility: X\n- Overall: X.X\n- Confidence: X\n\n### Meta-notes for Roundtable\n(1-2 sentences on what you most want to discuss with R2)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1177,"cache_read_input_tokens":53755,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1177},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3934af1-82f0-438e-bd39-6838b74091c7","uuid":"a90c9e3c-989a-4de8-85d4-de1e72aba272"}
