{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a1dffac903a89478a","tool_use_id":"toolu_011M3hibfofJrMQJmLC5Zuug","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":82730,"tool_uses":26,"duration_ms":140034},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"6b116511-f8f0-4446-8b5a-a698534cff70","session_id":"c3934af1-82f0-438e-bd39-6838b74091c7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EkSvHc7D6Cy8bmkKJn39Ew","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019kpcBHJCXB2unC7qsxRn7T","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"demo_calibrated_sim","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260513T153032","overall_score":"borderline accept (Findings)","report":"\n# R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n## Paper Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper investigates why calibration of LLM-based user simulators fails when attempting to correct dialect-dependent behavioral biases (AAVE and Indian English vs. Standard American English). Through controlled experiments on Qwen2.5-7B and 14B in the tau-bench retail domain, the authors propose a four-mechanism taxonomy of calibration failure modes for both inference-time (BCP) and training-time (DPO) interventions, and demonstrate that failure severity is scale-dependent: destructive side effects disappear at 14B while calibration itself remains ineffective at both scales.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Well-defined hypothesis framework.** The three-hypothesis structure (H1: dialect sensitivity, H2: gap reduction >=30%, H3: no harm >=80% retention) provides crisp pass/fail criteria that make claims falsifiable. The thresholds are stated upfront rather than post-hoc, which is good experimental practice.\n\n- **Appropriate and rigorous statistical methodology.** Non-parametric bootstrap with BCa correction (B=10,000) is the right choice for this setting: the data are episode-level proportions with modest sample sizes, and BCa handles skewness and bias that would invalidate normal-approximation CIs. All five core comparisons in Table 2 exclude zero, and the sign test for FM4 directional consistency (10/12, p=0.006) is a clean non-parametric test that does not depend on distributional assumptions.\n\n- **Honest treatment of base rates and limitations.** The paper commendably reports both absolute and relative values for the question_rate arm of FM2 (baseline 0.044, absolute shift -2.3pp despite 52.3% relative drop), acknowledging the low base rate. The limitations section is unusually candid about single model family, modest sample sizes, and domain specificity.\n\n- **Systematic diagnostic approach.** The single-dimension ablation variants (single_dim_question, single_dim_politeness) are well-designed controls that isolate FM2 (cross-dimension interference) from potential multi-constraint confounds. The vLLM LoRA serving bug discovery (15% vs 60% completion) and subsequent use of merged models demonstrates methodological rigor in ruling out evaluation artifacts.\n\n- **Multi-run replication for key claims.** FM3 (overcorrection rebound, 4/4 runs consistent), FM4 (10/12 directional consistency), and the 14B DPO+BCP experiment (4 runs x 6 variants x 20 tasks = 480 episodes) provide reasonable replication evidence for the strongest claims. The cross-scale comparison with a proper attenuation CI (14.7pp, CI [8.1, 21.6]) is a model for how to compare effect sizes across conditions.\n\n- **Clean taxonomy with distinct failure signatures.** Each of the four mechanisms has a qualitatively different signature (completion collapse vs. cross-dimension shift vs. opposite-direction rebound vs. gap sign reversal), making the taxonomy non-trivially structured rather than a list of symptoms. The scale-dependent severity matrix (Table 6) is an effective synthesis device.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n- **FM1 and FM2 rest on single-run data (n=19-20) at 7B.** The primary evidence for FM1 (global behavior suppression) and FM2 (cross-dimension interference) comes from mvp-004, a single run with n=19-20 per variant. While effect sizes are large (completion drops >59%, politeness shift -25.6%), the bootstrap CI for FM2 (Table 2) is computed from this single run's 20 episodes. Bootstrap does not create information that is not in the sample -- with n=20 and a single run, we cannot assess run-to-run variability for these mechanisms at 7B without BCP-only multi-run replication. The paper acknowledges this but still elevates FM1 and FM2 to taxonomic status equivalent to the multi-run FM3 and FM4. The bcp7b-multirun-001 experiment appears to be in progress but its results are not in the paper.\n\n- **FM4 comparison conflates two baselines with different properties.** The \"base BCP\" reference for FM4 comes from mvp-004 (single run, n=19-20, base model) while the \"DPO+BCP\" data comes from hgate-dpo003-merged (4 runs, n=80, DPO-merged model). The base model has baseline completion 36.8% while the DPO model has baseline completion 29.4%. This completion difference means the two conditions produce dialogues of systematically different length and structure. The gap reversal swing (+15.8pp) could be partially confounded by the different dialogue quality distributions rather than being purely a DPO-BCP interaction effect. A cleaner comparison would use the DPO model's own behavior *without* BCP as the reference, or at minimum, control for completion rate.\n\n- **Single model family limits generalizability of the taxonomy.** The paper frames its contribution as \"the first taxonomy of calibration failure modes\" -- a general claim. But the taxonomy is validated on Qwen2.5 only (7B and 14B). The attempted cross-family validation (Llama-3-8B at 5% completion, Mistral-7B at 0%) failed due to tool-calling limitations, not because of BCP. This means the taxonomy could reflect Qwen2.5-specific behavior (e.g., how Qwen processes system prompts, its specific instruction-tuning recipe) rather than general calibration failure modes. The title does not hedge this -- \"Why Calibration Fails\" implies generality that the evidence does not yet support.\n\n- **DPO preference pair construction is underspecified.** The paper reports 1,434 dialect-calibrated preference pairs but provides insufficient detail on how these were constructed. What makes a response \"preferred\" vs. \"rejected\"? Are the behavioral rate targets encoded in the preference signal? How were the pairs generated -- from tau-bench episodes, synthetic generation, or human annotation? The train_dpo.py script reads prompt/chosen/rejected triples but the data generation pipeline is not described. Without this, it is impossible to assess whether the DPO training target is well-aligned with the calibration objective, which is critical for interpreting FM4.\n\n- **The 30% gap reduction threshold (H2) is arbitrary and may be too stringent.** The paper does not justify why 30% relative gap reduction is the appropriate threshold. At 7B base BCP, the observed gap reduction is 25-28% -- tantalizingly close to 30%. At 14B, the best single-run gap reduction is 27.6% (CI [18.3, 35.2], which *includes* 30%). The entire \"BCP fails\" narrative hinges on this threshold. If the threshold were 25%, the conclusions would change materially. The paper should either justify the threshold from prior work or present results parameterically across threshold values.\n\n- **The \"safety-efficacy gap\" framing overstates the scale evidence.** Two data points (7B and 14B) from the same model family do not establish a \"gap\" in any robust sense -- they establish a difference between two specific models. The extrapolation to a general principle about scaling requires either more scale points (e.g., 3B, 7B, 14B, 32B) or cross-family evidence. The term \"gap\" implies a systematic relationship that two points cannot confirm.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1**: For the FM4 reversal comparison, have you computed what the politeness gap looks like for the DPO model *without* any BCP prompts (i.e., DPO baseline only)? Table in the evidence shows DPO baseline politeness is 0.502 vs. base model 0.559. This 5.7pp shift in baseline politeness could account for much of the apparent \"reversal\" if BCP operates relative to each model's own baseline distribution. Can you decompose the reversal into (a) baseline shift from DPO and (b) differential BCP response?\n\n- **Q2**: The bootstrap CIs for FM4 (Table 2) compare 80 DPO+BCP episodes against 19 base BCP episodes. How sensitive are these CIs to the asymmetric sample sizes? Have you verified with a permutation test or other approach that does not assume independence between the two groups?\n\n- **Q3**: How were the 1,434 DPO preference pairs constructed? Specifically: (a) what is the source of the preference signal (human judgment, automated metric, or LLM-as-judge)? (b) Do preferred responses encode specific dialect-appropriate behavioral rates, and if so, what rates? (c) Were the pairs generated from tau-bench episodes or independently?\n\n- **Q4**: The paper mentions the \"Overcorrected\" variant targets \"very low politeness (~5%).\" What exactly are the BCP prompt instructions for each variant? The specific prompt wording is critical for assessing whether FM3 (overcorrection rebound) reflects a general model property or an artifact of specific prompt phrasing.\n\n## Missing Experiments or Ablations\n\n- **BCP-only multi-run replication at 7B.** FM1 and FM2 are taxonomic-level claims based on a single run. Even 2-3 additional runs (as apparently planned in bcp7b-multirun-001) would substantially strengthen these claims or reveal them as high-variance artifacts.\n\n- **DPO-only evaluation without BCP.** The paper argues FM4 is an \"interaction effect\" but never shows DPO model performance without BCP in a controlled comparison. Running the DPO-merged model on baseline tasks (no calibration prompt) with 4 runs would isolate DPO's standalone effect on behavioral rates from the interaction.\n\n- **Threshold sensitivity analysis for H2.** Presenting H2 pass rates as a function of the gap reduction threshold (e.g., 10%, 20%, 25%, 30%, 40%) would show whether the \"failure\" is sharp or gradual, and whether there is any meaningful calibration signal even if the 30% bar is not met.\n\n- **BCP prompt ablation.** The paper tests 5 BCP variants but does not systematically vary prompt intensity or framing. A softer BCP prompt (\"slightly adjust politeness\") vs. the current direct rate injection could reveal whether FM1/FM3 are artifacts of overly aggressive prompt design.\n\n- **Cross-family validation on a model with adequate tool-calling.** GPT-4o-mini or a capable open model (e.g., Qwen2.5-32B, or a different family with strong tool-calling) would address the most significant limitation.\n\n## Scores\n\n- Soundness: 3.0\n- Excitement: 3.0\n- Reproducibility: 3\n- Overall: 3.0\n- Confidence: 4\n\n## Meta-notes for Roundtable\n\nThe central tension I want to discuss with R2 is whether the taxonomy-level claims (especially FM1 and FM2) can stand on single-run n=20 evidence, and whether the FM4 \"interaction reversal\" is genuinely an interaction effect or an artifact of the shifted DPO baseline. If R2 focuses on framing/novelty, I would push on whether \"the first taxonomy\" framing is warranted given single-family evidence -- a weaker but more defensible framing would be \"failure modes observed in Qwen2.5 calibration\" rather than a general taxonomy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":702,"cache_read_input_tokens":81283,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":702,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011M3hibfofJrMQJmLC5Zuug","session_id":"c3934af1-82f0-438e-bd39-6838b74091c7","uuid":"8ecf9d78-53cd-45cd-a812-a71310232668"}
