{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HYVy17d3pPadB4wazqwe8D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DaGP4C9qCc1AKMTJFKRZsf","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"demo_calibrated_sim","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260513T153032","overall_score":"borderline accept","report":"# R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n## Paper Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper investigates why calibrating LLM-based user simulators to correct dialect-dependent behavioral biases (AAVE and Indian English) fails under both prompt-based (BCP) and training-based (DPO) interventions. Through experiments on Qwen2.5-7B and 14B in the tau-bench retail domain, the authors propose a four-mechanism taxonomy of calibration failure modes (global behavior suppression, cross-dimension interference, overcorrection rebound, DPO-BCP interaction reversal) and show that all four are more severe at 7B but calibration remains ineffective at both scales. The central conceptual contribution is the \"safety-efficacy gap\": scaling eliminates destructive side effects without improving calibration effectiveness.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **FM4 (DPO-BCP interaction reversal) is genuinely novel.** No prior work documents this specific adversarial interaction between preference optimization and prompt-based calibration. The 10/12 directional consistency with p=0.006, the +15.8pp swing with CI [10.2, 21.8], and the statistically confirmed attenuation at 14B (14.7pp, CI [8.1, 21.6]) make this the paper's strongest and most original finding. This goes beyond what Lee et al. (2024) showed about DPO fragility---it demonstrates a *compositional* failure mode where two individually reasonable interventions interact destructively.\n\n- **The scale-dependent failure severity analysis is a real contribution.** The observation that BCP transitions from \"catastrophic over-response\" at 7B to \"selective ignoring\" at 14B is non-obvious and empirically well-supported. The safety-efficacy gap framing---scaling is necessary for safe deployment but insufficient for effective calibration---is a useful conceptual tool for the community, even if it rests on only two scale points within one model family.\n\n- **Rigorous statistical methodology for a negative-results paper.** Bootstrap CIs with BCa correction, sign tests for directional consistency, and transparent reporting of all individual runs (including the anomalous Run 2 at 14B) set a good standard. The paper does not cherry-pick and explicitly acknowledges when effects are weak (e.g., the question-rate baseline of 0.044 making the 52.3% relative drop in FM2 correspond to only 2.3pp absolute).\n\n- **The related work section is well-structured and honest about positioning.** Each failure mode is explicitly connected to a known phenomenon (FM1 to persona-accuracy trade-off, FM2 to bias spillover, FM4 to DPO fragility), which strengthens rather than weakens the paper---it shows the authors understand these are instantiations of broader patterns while arguing for the value of documenting them in this specific context.\n\n- **The extended related work draft (Section 2 full version) demonstrates thorough literature coverage across five relevant sub-areas.** The distinction between diagnosing dialect bias (Seshadri et al. 2026, Sim2Real 2026) and attempting to correct it (this paper) is clearly drawn and legitimate.\n\n- **Practical implications are well-articulated.** The paper does not just report failures---it identifies concrete constraints (dimensions are coupled, DPO and BCP must be evaluated jointly, scaling does not solve the problem) that genuinely inform future calibration research directions.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n- **FM1, FM2, and FM3 individually have limited novelty.** FM1 (completion collapse under persona prompts) is a task-oriented instantiation of the persona-accuracy trade-off already documented by Basil et al. (2025) and Hu et al. (2026). FM2 (cross-dimension interference) directly parallels the bias spillover effect of Paraschou et al. (2026) [note: the paper cites this as \"Gupta et al. 2026\" which is an author attribution error---the first author is Paraschou, not Gupta]. FM3 (overcorrection rebound) is the most novel of the three, but its disappearance at 14B weakens its generality. Taken individually, relabeling known phenomena as \"FM1\" and \"FM2\" in a new context is incremental. The value comes from the *combination* and the scale analysis, but the \"first taxonomy\" claim slightly oversells this.\n\n- **The \"first taxonomy\" claim needs qualification.** The paper claims to present \"the first taxonomy of calibration failure modes for LLM user simulators.\" This is technically true by construction---the specific intersection of calibration + user simulation + dialect is narrow enough that no one has taxonomized it before. But two of the four mechanisms (FM1, FM2) are already-named phenomena in adjacent literatures. A more precise claim would be \"the first systematic empirical analysis documenting how known alignment failure modes manifest and interact in dialect-aware user simulation calibration.\" The taxonomy is more of an organizing framework for empirical observations than a discovery of fundamentally new phenomena.\n\n- **Single model family is a significant limitation for a taxonomy paper.** A taxonomy implicitly claims generality. With only Qwen2.5 (7B and 14B), the authors cannot distinguish between Qwen-specific behaviors and general LLM calibration failure modes. The Llama-3-8B (5% completion) and Mistral-7B (0% completion) controls failed due to insufficient tool-calling capability, which is acknowledged, but this means the entire taxonomy rests on one model family. The scale-dependent pattern (two points, same family) could reflect Qwen2.5-specific architectural properties rather than general scaling behavior.\n\n- **Missing related work in multi-objective alignment and controllable generation.** The paper does not engage with the substantial literature on multi-objective optimization in alignment (e.g., Rame et al. 2024 on rewarded soups; Yang et al. 2024 on multi-objective RLHF; Zhou et al. 2024 on beyond one-preference-fits-all). FM2 (cross-dimension interference) is essentially a multi-objective optimization problem---calibrating multiple behavioral dimensions simultaneously---and the multi-objective alignment literature has developed techniques (Pareto optimization, reward model ensembles) that directly address this. The controllable text generation literature (Yang & Klein 2021 FUDGE; Dathathri et al. 2020 PPLM; Liu et al. 2021 DExperts) also proposes methods for multi-attribute control that could serve as alternatives to BCP. Omitting these bodies of work makes the \"implications\" section feel incomplete---the paper identifies that dimensions are coupled (FM2) without acknowledging that multi-attribute controllability is an active and partially solved problem.\n\n- **The safety-efficacy gap concept, while useful, is thinly supported.** It rests on exactly two data points (7B and 14B) from the same model family. The paper frames this as a general principle (\"scaling changes how calibration fails, not whether it fails\"), but the evidence base is too narrow for such a strong generalization. At minimum, a third scale point (e.g., 3B or 32B) or a second model family would be needed to claim this is a robust pattern rather than a Qwen2.5-specific observation.\n\n- **FM3 and FM1 disappearing at 14B complicates the taxonomy's utility.** If two of four failure modes are entirely capacity-gated and vanish at moderate scale, practitioners working with 14B+ models (increasingly the norm) would only encounter FM2 and a weakened FM4. This limits the practical relevance of half the taxonomy. The paper acknowledges this but could more directly address whether the taxonomy is primarily a characterization of small-model behavior.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n- **On FM2 and multi-objective alignment:** Have you considered whether multi-objective alignment techniques (e.g., Pareto reward optimization, reward model ensembles) could address the cross-dimension interference you document? The controllable generation literature has methods for independent attribute control---does the paper's framing as a \"failure mode\" rather than an \"unsolved multi-objective problem\" risk missing available solutions?\n\n- **On the author attribution error:** The paper cites \"Gupta et al. (2026)\" for the bias spillover work (arXiv:2602.16438), but the actual first author is Paraschou (Eva Paraschou, Line Harder Clemmensen, Sneha Das). Can you confirm this is a citation error and correct it before camera-ready?\n\n- **On FM3 mechanism:** The \"attractor basin\" explanation for overcorrection rebound is speculative. Do you have any evidence distinguishing this from a simpler hypothesis---that the 7B model simply fails to parse the extreme calibration target and defaults to its prior, which happens to be above the baseline due to the BCP prompt activating politeness-associated patterns?\n\n- **On the 30% H2 threshold:** The choice of 30% gap reduction as the success criterion is somewhat arbitrary. The best observed gap reduction is 27.6% (CI [18.3, 35.2])---the CI actually includes 30%. How sensitive are the paper's conclusions to this threshold choice? Would a 25% threshold change the narrative substantially?\n\n## Missing Related Work\n\n- **Multi-objective alignment:** Rame et al. (2024) \"Rewarded soups: towards Pareto-optimal alignment by interpolating weights fine-tuned on diverse rewards\" (NeurIPS 2024); Yang et al. (2024) \"Rewards-in-Context: Multi-objective Alignment of Foundation Models with Dynamic Preference Adjustment\" (ICML 2024). These directly address the problem underlying FM2---optimizing multiple behavioral objectives simultaneously.\n\n- **Controllable text generation with multi-attribute control:** Yang & Klein (2021) \"FUDGE: Controlled Text Generation With Future Discriminators\"; Dathathri et al. (2020) \"Plug and Play Language Models\"; Liu et al. (2021) \"DExperts: Decoding-Time Controlled Text Generation with Experts and Anti-Experts.\" These methods achieve multi-attribute controllability at decoding time and are directly relevant alternatives to BCP.\n\n- **Scaling laws for alignment:** Bai et al. (2022) \"Training a Helpful and Harmless Assistant with Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback\" documents how alignment properties change with scale; McKenzie et al. (2023) \"Inverse Scaling: When Bigger Isn't Better\" shows scale-dependent failure patterns. Both are relevant context for the safety-efficacy gap claim.\n\n- **Dialect NLP and sociolinguistic fairness in dialogue systems:** Blodgett et al. (2020) \"Language (Technology) is Power: A Critical Survey of Bias in NLP\" provides the foundational framing for studying dialect bias; Ziems et al. (2023) \"Multi-VALUE: A Framework for Cross-Dialectal English NLP\" offers dialect evaluation methodology. These are relevant for the sociolinguistic positioning.\n\n- **Persona consistency in dialogue:** Song et al. (2021) \"BoB: BERT Over BERT for Training Persona-based Dialogue Models from Limited Personalized Data\"; Zhang et al. (2018) \"Personalizing Dialogue Agents.\" The persona consistency literature is relevant to FM1 (where persona-like calibration prompts degrade other capabilities).\n\n## Scores\n\n- Soundness: 3.5\n- Excitement: 3.0\n- Reproducibility: 3\n- Overall: 3.0\n- Confidence: 4\n\n## Meta-notes for Roundtable\n\nThe key question I want to discuss with R1 is whether the single-model-family limitation is fatal for a paper claiming to present a \"taxonomy\"---taxonomies imply generality, and the evidence base is narrow. I find FM4 genuinely novel and well-evidenced, but FM1-FM3 are largely relabeled known phenomena, making the contribution uneven. If the paper were reframed as \"an empirical case study documenting calibration failures in Qwen2.5 user simulation\" rather than \"a taxonomy,\" the evidence-to-claim ratio would be more appropriate for Findings.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":703,"cache_read_input_tokens":64960,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":703,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0139bvowbJY2nBJ3A9LAYjKZ","session_id":"c3934af1-82f0-438e-bd39-6838b74091c7","uuid":"a3f74155-2bf5-4ccf-b223-c923f1c65822"}
